<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Gehrig is a 36-year-old former professional baseball player who comes for his yearly preventive visit four weeks after his last ALS clinic visit. The ankle sprain healed within three weeks and he wears the ankle-foot orthosis whenever he goes out. He uses a cane outdoors. His speech slurs a little when he is tired. He has been offered a desk role in city public service and plans to start next month. He wants the vaccines and checks that make sense for someone his age with ALS, and he asked to talk through his wishes for future care while he can do so easily. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 36</td></tr><tr><td>ALS</td><td>Limb onset; slight slurring when tired; orthosis and cane outdoors</td></tr><tr><td>Ankle</td><td>Healed</td></tr><tr><td>Work</td><td>Starting a desk role in public service next month</td></tr><tr><td>His request</td><td>Vaccines, checks, and a conversation about future care</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on the clinic tablet with voice entry and reviewed with him by Dr. Hollister. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Fair; strong in spirit</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Supported at home and by friends from the game; meets the ALS clinic social worker</td></tr><tr><td>Substance use</td><td>AUDIT-C 2; no drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never smoked</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Stretching and the home exercise plan from physical therapy most days; short walks with the cane</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Higher-calorie meals and two shakes a day, as the dietitian advised</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Seven to eight hours; no morning headaches or breathlessness lying flat</td></tr><tr><td>Falls</td><td>One fall in July with an ankle sprain; rails added on the stairs; orthosis worn outdoors</td></tr><tr><td>Safety at home</td><td>Feels safe; smoke alarm working; no firearm at home</td></tr><tr><td>Road safety</td><td>Seat belt; no longer drives because of his hand and ankle weakness</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent but slow; uses a button hook and adapted cutlery</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

